+
+## Voyage 2 — DOM identity and escape-key defects
+
+**Base commit:** end of Voyage 1.
+
+**Defect 1 — DOM identity loss on toggle:** The `render()` function in `js/app.js` does `dom.todoList.innerHTML = ''` and recreates all `<li>` elements from scratch on every state change. A real browser holds live DOM references; after `render()`, those references become detached. The in-harness suite re-queries after each action so it never notices. Cypress, however, holds live references and fails with "element has detached from DOM". The app spec says "toggling the class `completed` on its parent `<li>`" — this means in-place class mutation, not list reconstruction.
+
+**Fix direction (Crew):** Replace the full-list `innerHTML = ''` rebuild in `render()` with in-place mutation: find the existing `<li>` by `data-id`, toggle its `completed` class, update the checkbox `checked` state, and leave the DOM node in place. The full rebuild is only needed for add/delete/route-change operations.
+
+**Defect 2 — Escape doesn't discard edit under blur:** The escape keydown handler resets `editingId` and calls `render()`, but the blur event handler fires afterward and calls `editTodo(id, e.target.value)`, writing the typed text back. In a real browser, pressing Escape triggers blur on the focused input, so the typed value overwrites the original. The app spec says "If escape is pressed during the edit, the edit state should be left and any changes be discarded."
+
+**Fix direction (Crew):** Track an `escapePressed` flag (or similar) set in the keydown handler and checked in the blur handler so blur skips `editTodo()` when escape was the trigger.
